     Well, on a PC, one isn't using a terminal emulator, but it is
possible to redefine one's function keys to do macros:
ESC[0;59;"EaElbereth";13p would set F1, via MS-DOS's ANSI, to that
Elbereth enscription.  For reference:
59-68 F1->F10
84-93 Shift-F1->Shift-F10
94-103 Ctrl-F1->Ctrl-F10
104-113 Alt-F1->Alt-F10
120-129 Alt-1->Alt-0
16-25 Alt-Q->Alt-P (across keyboard)
30-38 Alt-A->Alt-L
44-50 Alt-Z->Alt-M
130 Alt-Hyphen
131 Alt-=
     All extended ASCII codes such as these are prefixed by a null byte
(0).  There are also codes for the number pad:
71    72    73
75          77
79    80    81
     Apologies for the computer-specific and referenceable technical
discussion.  It's not all that long, and it's relevant to Nethack.
     The second point is to remember that such macros are straight "send
the keys along" macros.  If something goes wrong, or you hit the key in
the wrong place, nothing's going to abort the macro.  For instance, if
you had typed "P", the macro would put on ring E if it existed (if
you're lucky, you'll get a "--more-- prompt along the way, and all the
rest of the input will be dumped), apply E (at which point you'll
probably get a "--more--" prompt even if E is a ring), and then send
along the rest of "Elbereth".  If you're lucky, E wasn't a cursed ring. 
There are probably more disastrous possibilities.  So use such features
with care.  (This is where a Nethack macro comes in useful, since it can
abort at any point deemed worthwhile.)
     It might be nice if one could hack in an option for experienced
MS-DOS users that would accept a line of input at the top of the screen
and then dump it, prefixed by an ESC, as output.  That way we could
easily create ANSI macros on the fly.  One would have to do it every
time one started the game, of course, unless Nethack (PC only, of
course) accepted an input file full of lines to dump to output, prefixed
by ESCs.  Not very user-friendly, but very functional.  One could also
use the input file to change the screen color, set the screen mode (with
NANSI or other ANSI alternatives, 43-line mode as well), and so forth. 
I have a file that describes how to use ANSI commands that could be
included with the distribution as well, although I'm sure the PC
development team can access such files themselves.

